hi ben and stefan,

i totally agree, that 's why i wrote "(to some extent)" :-( the problem
really is "where do we draw the line"?

i do think it would be great to come to an intial consensus about this. i guess, easiest would be to take "other" disciplines as an
example, so shall we start there? we can then go on to discussing issues of independence and breaking our
heads about operationalization ...

basically: i don't see how we can justify applying weaker selection procedures than other disciplines while fitting simpler models.
to me it
*seems* that allowing only one example per author resembles the
procedures followed in other sciences best, i.e. one answer per subject
but multiple subjects from the same age group etc. if we don't do *at
least* that, we definitely shouldn't use the basic models. it is "not
done" in "other" discplines either: as soon as they have multiple
people doing multiple things, corrections are in order. this *doesn't* mean we can't use multiple examples from one subject, it
"just" means we should take the multiple measurements per subject into account
when analyzing the data. you might still find that there is no
subject-effect, in which case you can run the simpler model, i guess. but i
think, to be safe, you should first check. this also *doesn't* mean that taking one example per subject guarantees independence ... i think many of the things stefan list *might* be issues, eg A taking up material from B, or A replying to B ...

anyone any more cents to spare?
dagmar

